  "text": "Let's first label the points and circles for better understanding:\n\n1. The cylinder is placed upside down on the plane.\n2. The wire touches the cylinder at a point, and it makes an angle of 60 degrees with the horizontal plane.\n3. A sphere is placed 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane, and it just touches the wire.\n\nFirst, we find the length of the wire using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Length of the wire} &= \\sqrt{10^2 + r^2} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the cylinder's base.\n\nSince the wire makes an angle of 60 degrees with the plane, we can use trigonometry to find the height of the cylinder:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Height of the cylinder} &= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot r \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe know that the wire touches the sphere, which is located 10 cm away from the point where the wire touches the plane. Therefore, we can find the radius of the sphere using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Radius of the sphere} &= \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{200} \\\\\n&= 10 \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can use the trigonometry formula to find the radius of the base of the cylinder:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Height} &= \\tan 60^\\circ \\cdot \\text{Radius of the cylinder} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3} \\cdot r &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 10 \\\\\nr &= \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{10 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{20 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nr &\\approx 11.547 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the radius of the cylinder's base is approximately 11.547 cm.",
